<title>UK: Brown says no need for pension contribs to rise.</title>
<headline>Brown says no need for pension contribs to rise.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-07-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>British Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown said on Thursday the corporate tax changes he announced in his budget on Wednesday did not represent a &quot;raid&quot; on pension funds.</p>
<p>&quot;The changes in the tax credits on pension funds, like the cut in corporation tax, are to get long-term investment,&quot; he told GMTV.</p>
<p>Asked if millions of people would have to make higher contributions to their pensions as a result, he replied:</p>
<p>&quot;This is nonsense. It's not correct. The pension funds have very big surpluses, indeed many companies have got pension holidays at the moment.&quot;</p>
<p>Brown said he had kept his pre-election promises to spend more on health and education and not to raise income tax.</p>
<p>-- Richard Murphy, London Newsroom 44 171 542 2774</p>
</text>
